d meaningful predictive power.\ndf.drop(columns=['ZipCode'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column BankCustomer is dropped\n# The BankCustomer feature may overlap with other variables such as Income, CreditScore, and Debt, which encapsulate an individual's financial activity more comprehensively.\ndf.drop(columns=['BankCustomer'], inplace=True)\nend", "### Step 1: Interaction Feature Between Prior Default and Employment  \npython\n# prior_employed_interaction\ndf['prior_employed_interaction'] = df['PriorDefault'] * df['Employed']\n# Feature description\n# This feature captures the interaction between having prior defaults and current employment status. It identifies individuals with a prior credit default but are currently employed, which might reflect a recovery or improvement in financial stability.\n# Usefulness: Provides insight into whether employment status offsets the risks associated with a prior default, useful for predicting successful credit applications.\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Debt-to-Income Ratio  \npython\n# debt_to_income_ratio\ndf['debt_to_income_ratio'] = df['Debt'] / (df['Income'] + 1e-5)\n# Feature description\n# This feature calculates the ratio of outstanding debt to income, providing a measure of financial burden relative to earning capacity.\n# Usefulness: People with a higher debt-to-income ratio are generally less likely to be approved for a credit card due to financial stress. This indicator can be a strong predictor of creditworthiness.\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Has Credit History  \npython\n# has_credit_history\ndf['has_credit_history'] = (df['CreditScore'] > 0).astype(int)\n# Feature description\n# This feature creates a binary indicator to differentiate individuals who have any credit history (CreditScore > 0) from those who do not.\n# Usefulness: Credit history is often a critical factor in approving credit card applications. This feature highlights individuals with and without historical credit activity.\nend