ize variance, especially given potential outliers in the dataset.\n# Usefulness: Debt-to-Income ratio is a key indicator of financial stress. The log transformation further normalizes its distribution, making the feature more interpretable for models.\nend\n\n---\n\npython\n# Employment_CreditScore_Product\ndf['Employment_CreditScore_Product'] = df['Employed'] * df['CreditScore']\n# Feature description\n# This feature creates a new interaction metric by combining 'Employed' and 'CreditScore'.\n# Usefulness: It highlights the contribution of a high credit score among employed individuals, which could indicate financial responsibility and stability, and thus better creditworthiness.\nend\n\n---\n\npython\n# Married_Debt_to_Income_Interaction\ndf['Married_Debt_to_Income_Interaction'] = df['Married'] * (df['Debt'] / (df['Income'] + 1e-5))\n# Feature description\n# This feature multiplies marital status with the Debt-to-Income ratio to capture potential joint financial responsibility impacts.\n# Usefulness: Married individuals with a high Debt-to-Income ratio may exhibit different financial habits or creditworthiness patterns compared to single individuals. This interaction can add meaningful variance for the model.\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropping Uninformative or Redundant Columns:\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column ZipCode is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['ZipCode'], inplace=True)\n# ZipCode may provide coarse geographic information, but it isn't directly tied to financial behavior, creditworthiness, or risk management. It risks introducing bias without offering significant predictive value.\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column DriversLicense is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['DriversLicense'], inplace=True)\n# The DriversLicense column does not provide any relevant financial information about an individual’s credit behavior or risk. Other features like income, employment, or credit score better capture credit card approval predictors.\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why